    "case.idx-20726.tag": "Rare legitimate case",
    "case.idx-20726.detail": "A $2,425,000 loan against a $3,505,000 property and $548,000 income in California. Every figure is large, but they scale together — loan-to-value and loan-to-income both stay in a normal range for a high-net-worth buyer. Flagged by 2 of 3 methods purely for magnitude, not for being internally inconsistent.",
    "case.idx-20309.tag": "Data-quality artifact",
    "case.idx-20309.detail": "Reported income of 8,888 (in thousands) is HMDA's own \"not applicable\" sentinel code, not a real $8.9M income — and property_value lands at exactly $554,168.53, this dataset's imputed placeholder mean to the cent. Two independent data-quality tells on one record, flagged by all 3 methods. This is the clearest case in the whole flagged set of an anomaly detector correctly catching a data artifact, not a risky loan.",
    "case.idx-97395.tag": "Potential risk signal",
    "case.idx-97395.detail": "An $85,000 subordinate-lien loan for \"other purpose\" against a $1,005,000 property — a small second loan riding on a very expensive property, and denied. Unlike the two cases above, there's no data-quality tell here: the numbers are internally consistent, just an unusual structure on a declined application. The closest thing in this flagged set to a genuine underwriting-review candidate.",

    // ---- phase 5: findings & recommendations ----
    "p5.callout.q": "The central question this phase exists to answer",
    "p5.callout.a": "Anyone can run a pivot table and see that most applications get approved, or that California and Texas have the most volume. That's obvious from the raw file. What follows is <b>not</b> — each finding below required combining segmentation, pattern mining, and anomaly detection, then checking them against each other.",
    "p5.finding.1": "<b>Purchased loans are structurally missing demographic data</b> — found independently by K-Means, Apriori (lift 11.0–12.5×, confidence 87–97%), and the rule network. Three unrelated methods agreeing makes this a discovery, not a data quirk.",
    "p5.finding.2": "<b>Many \"extreme\" anomalies were missing-data artifacts, not real outliers.</b> 71.5% of the strictest (all-3-methods) flagged anomalies were the imputed-mean placeholder, found by cross-checking Phase 1's imputation against Phase 4's output — correcting two Phase 2 segment profiles.",
    "p5.finding.3": "<b>Withdrawn applications carry the same missing-demographics signature as purchased loans, for a different reason</b> — the highest-lift rule found anywhere (13.22×), pointing to a disclosure-process friction point, not the secondary-market pattern.",
    "p5.finding.4": "<b>The applicant population has no natural density-based clusters — it's a continuum.</b> DBSCAN (ε = 5.7) found 99.4% of applicants form one connected mass; only 0.6% are genuinely isolated. K-Means' 4 segments are a useful <i>imposed</i> grouping, not a structure the data was \"waiting to reveal.\"",
    "p5.rec.1": "<b>Exclude or separately flag purchased loans</b> before computing any fair-lending or demographic-outcome metric — their missing data will otherwise silently distort the results.",
    "p5.rec.2": "<b>Investigate the withdrawal/disclosure process</b> for medium-to-high-value property applications; the pattern found here suggests a specific friction point, not random attrition.",
    "p5.rec.3": "<b>Treat debt-to-income above 60% as a confirmed, broad underwriting signal</b> — it held up across every segment checked against it.",
    "p5.rec.4": "<b>Route the \"high interest rate + denial/thin serviceability\" flagged applications to underwriting</b> for manual pattern review; this is the most business-relevant anomaly category found.",
    "p5.rec.5": "<b>Audit the missing-value imputation step</b> in any pipeline built on this dataset — checking for the exact mean-placeholder value is cheap and should be a standard data-quality check before reporting segment-level financial statistics.",
    "p5.rec.6": "<b>Report K-Means segments as an applied business grouping, not a discovered natural structure</b> — pair any segment-level claim with the DBSCAN continuum finding (only 0.6% isolated) so stakeholders don't over-read the 4 segments as real population boundaries.",
    "p5.rec.src.finding1": "→ Finding #1", "p5.rec.src.finding2": "→ Finding #2", "p5.rec.src.finding3": "→ Finding #3", "p5.rec.src.finding4": "→ Finding #4",
    "p5.rec.src.phase3denial": "→ Phase 3, denial-reason rules", "p5.rec.src.phase4case": "→ Phase 4, anomaly case review",
    "p5.recs.hint": "Ordered to match the 4 findings above — Recs #1–#4 map 1:1 to Findings #1–#4 — plus two extra action items (#5, #6) from patterns in Phase 3's rules and Phase 4's case review that didn't make the 4-finding headline cut on their own.",
